  The secret to Rogues is collecting daggers and throwing them 
*early* and often.  (Hint: what's good about hitting monsters 
that can't immediately hit you back?  ;^)  Daggers can even be 
thrown at melee ranges.


  Rogues with expert level dagger skills can throw up to four
daggers per firing.  That's an average of 2.5 daggers per throw.


  Rogues start with 6 to 15 uncursed +0 <racial> daggers.  
They're a stack.  You can 'f'ire them without unwielding
your favorite melee weapon.  Daggers never just disappear
from hitting a monster.


  Damaged daggers don't stack with undamaged or less/more
damaged daggers.  (HINT:  Don't throw your daggers where they'll
get damaged unless absolutely necessary. ;^)


  Your big limitation is the number of daggers in a given stack.
You can't throw more daggers than are currently in the stack 
you're throwing.


  #adjust your biggest/best stack of daggers to 'Q', for easy
manual quivering: "QQ".   #Adjust your second biggest/best stack 
to 'R'. (Autoquiver searches for quiverable weapons in the order 
'a-zA-Z'.) (Yes, I got the order wrong in an earlier post.)


  Daggers can be heavy in large numbers.  Dump something 
non-battle critical if necessary.  Throwing daggers at an
approaching monster (at a distance) can even get you unburdened 
by the time the monster reaches you (*if* it does :-), especially
when you're averaging 2.5 daggers per fling.

  You can almost always go back to get something you've stashed
when needed, if you character is still alive.  (Dead characters
don't ascend, either.)  Killing monsters quickly at range is a 
great help in keeping characters alive.

  I can't find Rogues starting skill level with daggers in any
of the role/class spoilers.  But there are so many separate 
role/class spoilers that it's easy to miss one.


  Worst case Rogues start unskilled with daggers.  Fortunately,
daggers are easiest weapon for an early character to build up
skill in from scratch.  Because all daggers get a +2 to hit 
bonus.  (I presume this includes Magicbane.)


  A Rogue with a big stack of blessed, highly enchanted 
damage-proof daggers and expert daggers skill isn't just a 
killing machine, it's a *ranged* killing machine.


  #twoweapon with Magicbane and a silver saber is pretty good
as well.  (Enchant to Magicbane to +6/7, you'll still get curse
absorbtion, magic resistance, and Elbereth engraving, some magic
effects, and about four more points of damage per hit over 
Magicbane at +2.)  

