  The MR column is the monster's intrinsic magic resistance.  
This is used in a less than straightforward formula to determine
the probability of the monster actually resisting an attempted
magical effect.

  NB, not all of these monsters have anything to cancel.  Some
may technically be cancelable, but not worth doing so.

 e.g. (Master) Mind Flayers can be cancelled, but only the 
telepathic attack is affected, not the brain sucking attack.
So to make effective use of a successful cancel attempt on
a given monster it can be *very* important to understand 
exactly what is cancelled on each monster.

  A canceled monster is rarely as good as dead.  (Dead monsters
are usually as good as canceled, though. ;^)

  So some of the monsters in the above table may already be 
included in the count of those not worth canceling with 
MagicBane's melee range cancel.


  Increasing the damage Magicbane does means weaker motives
for switching to a more "effective" (i.e. greater damage 
inflicting) weapon.  And Magicbane's big strengths: curse 
absorption and magic resistance, are only obtained by 
wielding it.  They are also completely unaffected by 
the enchantment level.



  Percentage of time wielding Magicbane is most important
for surprise "cursing attacks."  These occur most on the
ascension run.  (Although players unaware of which particular
monsters also have inventory cursing attacks could well
benefit from wielding Magicbane as much as possible.)




